WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Billy Loomis was born in 1978 or 1979 as the&nbsp;
son of Henry Loomis and Nancy Loomis. Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.500
was once a lawyer for the movie studio Sunrise&nbsp;
Studios, but in April 1974, after helping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.920
studio escape from a lawsuit regarding one&nbsp;
of their actresses going missing on set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.120
he left the Studio and moved up north -- to&nbsp;
Woodsboro, California, where he met Nancy.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.480
Unfortunately, he could not leave his&nbsp;
scandalous Hollywood life in the rearview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.260
because after Billy was born, he had an&nbsp;
affair with a woman who also fled Sunrise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:30.120
Studios for a quieter life in Woodboro. Her&nbsp;
name was Maureen Prescott. Little did he know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.600
Maureen was quite a tramp. She&nbsp;
had a secret-son named Roman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.680
who was secretly watching and video taping&nbsp;
their broad-daylight encounters in cheap motels.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.340
When Billy’s mother found out about the&nbsp;
infidelity, she split up with Henry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.820
skipped town, leaving behind Billy with&nbsp;
his father. As Billy describes himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.260
maternal abandonment causes serious deviant&nbsp;
behavior. However, he managed to keep a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.980
respectable public persona and seems to be&nbsp;
fairly popular in school. He had developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.120
a love of movies, perhaps partially intrigued&nbsp;
by his Dad’s former career at Sunrise Studios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.940
and he has a group of friends&nbsp;
who share this passion with him.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.240
Because of his obsession with cinema and&nbsp;
his inability to cope with abandonment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.340
he’s an easy target when he meets Roman sometime&nbsp;
in 1994, who was now an up and coming music video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.360
director who similarly could not get over&nbsp;
being forgotten by his mom. He showed Billy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.140
the footage of Maureen and Hank having the&nbsp;
affair, and even helped him plot his revenge.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.560
Because Roman was a director, looking&nbsp;
to get into feature films in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.560
his ideas for Billy were very much&nbsp;
like something out of a movie:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.040
such as having a partner to sell out in case&nbsp;
he got caught, and finding someone else to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.240
frame for his crimes. Billy chose his easily&nbsp;
peer-pressured friend Stu Macher to be his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.200
accomplice and decided to frame the subject&nbsp;
of Maureen’s newest affair, Cotton Weary.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.600
But getting his revenge on Maureen was not enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.440
he also wanted to hurt her daughter,&nbsp;
Sidney Prescott. Billy is considered an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.100
attractive guy in the high school social&nbsp;
sphere. Tatum Riley calls him flawless…

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.640
– He was destined to have a&nbsp;
flaw. I knew he was too perfect.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.900
–[Zac] ...and other girls at the school gossip about&nbsp;
his bubble butt. For these reasons, it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.920
hard for him to get Sidney to date him, and the&nbsp;
relationship started off hot and heavy. But that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.320
would change when Billy’s plan went into action&nbsp;
in September 1995. One night, Billy and Stu stole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.580
Cotton Weary’s coat and violated Maureen before&nbsp;
putting an end to her life. One of them left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.540
the house wearing Cotton’s coat, and made sure&nbsp;
to leave in such a way that Sidney got a vague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.280
glimpse, enough to identify the jacket and nothing&nbsp;
else. Perhaps Billy put the jacket over his head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.320
as he loudly left, while Stu made a quieter escape&nbsp;
and hung low in a hole in the surrounding woods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.580
A woods burrow, if you will. Then Billy&nbsp;
planted the jacket in Cotton Weary’s car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.300
and that was more than enough to get Sidney&nbsp;
to testify against Cotton as an eye-witness.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.740
But Billy and Stu could not just leave it at&nbsp;
that after their success. To learn how Billy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.940
took his love of horror movies too far, why&nbsp;
he has facial hair in the poster despite him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.720
never having that in the movie, he modeled his&nbsp;
killings after his favorite fictional characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.400
and how he was inspired by a real life&nbsp;
criminal, stick around to the end of this video.

00:02:44.611 --> 00:02:46.611
(Metal music)

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.980
Do you like scary movies? Good. Welcome to&nbsp;
Horror History. Billy Loomis was named after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.300
two iconic horror movie characters whose movies&nbsp;
defined their generation. From the Boomers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.400
there’s Sam Loomis, Marion Crane’s boyfriend&nbsp;
in Psycho. Generation X had Dr. Samuel Loomis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.180
the psychiatrist of Michael Myers in&nbsp;
Halloween. Billy Loomis would carry the torch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.860
because Scream is a cornerstone for Generation Y.&nbsp;
The tradition would be taken one step further in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.940
Billy’s eventual daughter, whose name is Samantha.&nbsp;
She ends up taking on the last name of her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.800
but if she had taken her father’s name, she would&nbsp;
be yet another Sam Loomis. Additionally, Billy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.180
would steal elements of both Halloween and Psycho&nbsp;
for his own crime spree, which we’ll get to.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.980
Billy is portrayed by Skeet Ulrich, who is clean&nbsp;
shaven for the entire film, despite what the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.240
poster would have you believe, and also he wears&nbsp;
this shiny leather jacket for some reason. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.560
shooting Scream, Skeet played a part in the 1997&nbsp;
film As Good As It Gets, where his character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.820
Vincent, has facial hair. This is when they did&nbsp;
the promotional shots for Scream, so he couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.860
shave and mess up the continuity in As Good As It&nbsp;
Gets. Ironically, for Skeet, Scream was as good as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:54.960
it gets career-wise. Also, Photoshop did exist at&nbsp;
this time, but they decided not to use it I guess.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.560
Screenwriter Kevin Williamson was partially&nbsp;
inspired by The Gainesville Ripper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.700
a criminal believed to have taken 8 victims&nbsp;
in the latter half of 1990. Other than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.420
fact that many of the victims were students,&nbsp;
there isn’t much resemblance to Billy Loomis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.080
but what is similar is the relationship&nbsp;
between the killers and the media. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.440
media was accused of exploiting Gainville&nbsp;
and creating a moral panic in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.580
get more attention on their story. However, the&nbsp;
killer was doing it all for media attention. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.960
wanted to be a celebrity like Ted Bundy, even&nbsp;
breaking into song during his eventual trial.

00:04:23.173 --> 00:04:28.020
– ♪ I recall the day I first saw you. ♪

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.800
– The media was feeding him with coverage,&nbsp;
and he was feeding them with content. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.320
Gainesville Ripper is not the only&nbsp;
criminal we can say this about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.600
but he is the one cited by the writer of Scream.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.520
The Scream franchise has Gale Weathers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:42.150
a tabloid reporter who is unapologetic about&nbsp;
her motivation for catching the killer.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.920
– Do you know what that&nbsp;
could do for my book sales?

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.300
– The aspect of the media exploiting the crimes&nbsp;
as entertainment also comes from the Gainesville&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.320
Ripper. During his trial, he claimed to have&nbsp;
multiple personality disorder and blamed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.760
crimes on one of the personalities, named Gemini.&nbsp;
This is a reference to The Gemini Killer from The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.360
Exorcist III, the spirit of a serial killer&nbsp;
who possessed Father Karras.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.560
“It was revealed that Rolling saw ″The Exorcist&nbsp;
III″ while in Gainesville, possibly hours before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.420
he began his murder spree,” reported Ron Word from&nbsp;
AP News. So that’s where Williamson got the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.800
for Billy Loomis to incorporate his love of horror&nbsp;
movie tropes into his plan, and he would do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.660
at nearly every level. But before I unpack that,&nbsp;
I’ve gotta talk about Billy’s secret girlfriend.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.140
(mysterious music)

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.820
After the disposal of Maureen&nbsp;
Prescott went off without a hitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.000
Sidney was experiencing post traumatic&nbsp;
stress which deterred her from getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.640
physical with her boyfriend. In&nbsp;
order to keep her on his hook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.600
he pretends to be patient and understanding&nbsp;
with her for nearly a year after the incident.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.920
In order to satisfy his teenage libido,&nbsp;
he goes behind Sid’s back and gets with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.160
girl named Christina Carpenter, a fellow&nbsp;
high school student who ends up getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.340
pregnant with Billy’s kid. Christina&nbsp;
has her own boyfriend at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.480
who believed he was the father. Billy’s&nbsp;
affair is just like Hank and Maureen’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.900
and his willingness to follow in his father’s&nbsp;
footsteps despite knowing the damage that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:59.160
can cause is a testament to his narcissism.&nbsp;
Billy would not be around to see the birth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:04.380
his daughter, because he and Stu had plans for the&nbsp;
anniversary of Maureen’s death in September 1996.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.760
Their first target for that year would be Stu’s&nbsp;
ex-girlfriend Casey Becker, who is also a fan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.080
horror movies and familiar with horror tropes. In&nbsp;
order to make their story feel worthy of cinemas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.920
they take inspiration from popular slasher&nbsp;
villains at the time. Leatherface, Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.720
Voorhees and Michael Myers were all known for&nbsp;
wearing a mask, so Billy and Stu did the same. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.400
particular, the events of Scream line up most with&nbsp;
Halloween, which is Casey’s favorite scary movie.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.300
In Halloween, Michael Myers wears a randomly&nbsp;
selected white mask that he stole from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.900
Hardware Store, so Billy and Stu do the&nbsp;
same by getting these Father Death masks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.240
which can be purchased at any&nbsp;
five-and-dime store in the state.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.520
They wait outside Casey’s house one night and&nbsp;
stumble upon her new boyfriend Steve Orth who is on his way over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.800
so they beat him up, tie him up, and hide him&nbsp;
away before making a call to Casey’s house.

00:06:47.482 --> 00:06:48.978
(phone rings)

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.430
(impact)

00:06:55.246 --> 00:06:59.218
(suspenseful music)

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:03.420
I'm guessing that Billy was the caller using a&nbsp;
voice changer device to speak with Casey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.520
not Stu. It’s Billy’s plan, and he’s definitely&nbsp;
the wittier of the two. I’m *not* sure whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.360
phone they might have used, maybe Steve Orth had&nbsp;
a cell phone that they stole after abducting him.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.800
When Casey picks up, he flirts with her at first,&nbsp;
then strikes fear into her when he asks her name.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.700
– Because I want to know who I’m looking at.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.600
– She gets freaked out and starts hanging up on&nbsp;
him each time he calls, until he threatens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:28.260
gut her like a fish if she doesn’t stay on.&nbsp;
He watches the house from his hiding place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:31.740
in the front while Stu waits out of sight&nbsp;
with Steve Orth closer to the back door.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.160
I feel like the fact that there are two&nbsp;
Ghostfaces further supports the throughline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.760
that they’re creating a horror movie. Most&nbsp;
of the slashers that inspired them had some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.020
kind of supernatural element, and by having two&nbsp;
people in the same costume, they could give Casey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:46.740
the impression that Ghostface is teleporting&nbsp;
between the front and back of the house. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.940
villain may be ringing the doorbell one moment,&nbsp;
and placing Steve on the back patio the next.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.900
He challenges Casey to a game of horror movie&nbsp;
trivia, but the second question is a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.160
misleading, causing Casey to get it wrong,&nbsp;
and Steve to be taken out as a result. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.240
third question again plays with the fact that she&nbsp;
doesn’t know there are two of them. He asks “what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:08.160
door am I at” and when she refuses to answer, they&nbsp;
break in and search the house. In my estimation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.660
Stu is the one to stab his ex-girlfriend, just&nbsp;
based on her look of understanding, but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:17.220
surprise after she sees the killer unmasked.&nbsp;
Together, they hang her in a tree and gut her.

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:21.360
Later that night, Billy stops by Sidney’s house&nbsp;
and climbs through her window. She makes him hide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.140
from her dad, who comes in and explains when he’ll&nbsp;
be leaving the next morning and where he’ll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:29.160
staying out of town over the weekend. This time&nbsp;
around, Billy is planning to frame Neil for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.300
murders, so overhearing this information is like&nbsp;
striking gold, and he remembers it for later.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.860
– Close call!

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.280
– He tells Sidney about how he was&nbsp;
at home and saw The Exorcist on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.760
with all the good stuff censored for television,&nbsp;
and it made him think of their relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.180
which cooled off after Mrs. Prescott’s&nbsp;
death. It’s significant that he’s making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:49.680
an analogy to The Exorcist, because remember,&nbsp;
The Gainesville Reaper lifted his story from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.580
movie in The Exorcist franchise as well, which&nbsp;
he supposedly watched right before going crazy.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:57.720
He claims he’s not trying to rush into sleeping&nbsp;
together, and as he leaves, she asks if he’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:02.520
settle for a PG-13 relationship. His words are&nbsp;
empty ones, but he really only has himself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.720
blame for Sidney’s hesitancy to get intimate in&nbsp;
the wake of her Mom’s funeral. He secretly does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.040
want to go further with her, but only because it&nbsp;
fits into his sick fantasy of living out a horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.940
movie. Everyone knows losing your virginity&nbsp;
is a precursor to death in the horror genre.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:18.960
Early the next morning, Billy and Stu capture&nbsp;
Mr. Prescott and hide him away in Stu’s house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.280
because his parents are out of town as well. They&nbsp;
park his car in the woods nearby and also steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.880
his cell phone, which they clone so that each&nbsp;
of them can make calls in order to frame him.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:31.020
At school that day, Billy is interrogated by&nbsp;
police, just like all the Woodsboro High students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.420
and apparently they ask him if&nbsp;
he likes to hunt. After class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.020
they’re all sitting out in the quad eating&nbsp;
grapes and talking about what happened to Casey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:41.520
and Billy gets mad at Stu for lacking tact while&nbsp;
discussing how she was gutted. It looks like he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.420
standing up for his squeamish girlfriend, but&nbsp;
he’s really just playing the part of innocence.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:49.800
That evening, Billy knows Sidney is home alone,&nbsp;
having taken care of both of her parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.920
and hides in her house while she’s&nbsp;
sleeping upstairs. He calls her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:56.340
using the cloned cell phone and after&nbsp;
asking about her favorite scary movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.240
challenges her to find him, which eventually&nbsp;
leads to him busting out of her closet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.260
chasing her up to her room, where she barricades&nbsp;
herself. Despite going after her with a knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:07.800
it is possible that he intended to let&nbsp;
her survive this encounter. He doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.160
take his chance to cut her throat, and&nbsp;
he’s almost too clumsy during the chase.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:15.480
Plus, he still wanted to take her virginity&nbsp;
before revealing himself as the killer in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:20.220
to further humiliate her, and he would have wanted&nbsp;
someone at some point to survive an encounter in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:25.140
order to tell the tale and elevate the status of&nbsp;
Ghostface into a horror icon. For these reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.560
he ditches the costume near her front door&nbsp;
and goes around to climb into her window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.800
in street clothes, pretending to be the&nbsp;
concerned boyfriend coming to the rescue.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.553
– He’s gone. He’s gone. It’s alright.

00:10:37.553 --> 00:10:41.280
— How would you know that? You haven’t checked&nbsp;
the house yet. To add further suspicion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.500
he drops his phone, and Sidney immediately makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:47.040
the connection -- it wasn’t very common for&nbsp;
a teenager to own a mobile phone at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.000
though it was maybe starting to get popular&nbsp;
based on Billy’s dialogue at the police station.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:55.320
Also, why would the killer abandon his costume&nbsp;
before fleeing unless he was planning to go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:59.040
and be someone else? It doesn’t look too&nbsp;
good for Billy here, and he’s arrested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.300
but lucky for him, his dad was a lawyer, and he&nbsp;
begs the sheriff to call him as they book him.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:05.220
Sheriff Burke does call Mr. Loomis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.940
so Billy is able to use his one phone&nbsp;
call at the police station to contact Stu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:13.080
who has the other clone of Neil Prescott’s phone&nbsp;
and the other voice changer, and instruct him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.780
call Sidney at the Riley household where&nbsp;
she’ll she’ll be staying later that night.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.860
In the meantime, he glares at Sidney through the&nbsp;
interrogation room window, taking advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:24.180
the intimate small town police station to try to&nbsp;
make her feel bad for him and deflect suspicion.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.760
After a short interrogation,&nbsp;
they move him to a holding cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.220
and his dad encourages him to&nbsp;
keep his mouth shut for now.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:30.658
– Wait for the lawyer Billy.

00:11:30.658 --> 00:11:34.920
Hank is an entertainment lawyer, not a criminal&nbsp;
defense attorney, but I’m sure he has connections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.240
However, that ends up not really being necessary&nbsp;
because Stu’s Ghostface call to Sidney works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:43.620
like a charm, nobody suspects there being 2&nbsp;
killers. The police also check his phone records,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:46.980
which are clean, so he’s released the next&nbsp;
morning, and even has time to change his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:51.240
clothes and style his hair… but apparently&nbsp;
not wash the fingerprint ink off of his hands?

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.720
Maybe he just decided to leave that in&nbsp;
order to make Sidney feel bad. He runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:58.020
into her in the halls and she’s still&nbsp;
pretty shaken up. He tries way too hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:01.980
to convince her of his innocence, despite her&nbsp;
confirmation that she no longer suspects him.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:05.670
– I know, the cops said I scared&nbsp;
him away. It wasn’t me Sid.&nbsp;

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:09.300
– I know. He called me again&nbsp;
last night at Tatum’s house.&nbsp;

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.660
– See? It couldn’t have been&nbsp;
me, I was in jail, remember?

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:17.160
– He also chooses this moment as a really bad&nbsp;
time to try to guilt her into taking their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:20.280
physical relationship beyond&nbsp;
PG-13, telling her that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:23.520
time to get over her mother’s death,&nbsp;
which understandably pisses her off.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.387
– Stupid!

00:12:25.387 --> 00:12:29.040
– The only reason he tried to put a little pressure&nbsp;
on her here is because they’re only a day away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:32.820
from the 1-year anniversary of the Maureen&nbsp;
Prescott murder, so if he’s going to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:37.980
her v-card before taking her l-card… her life…&nbsp;
he’s gotta do it to in the next 40 hours or so.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.060
After school, we see Stu leave with Sidney and&nbsp;
Tatum, but he does kind of go back towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:45.840
school, so it’s basically 50/50 on which one&nbsp;
of them was the one to kill Principal Himbry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:49.680
and lean over him with the Ghostface mask&nbsp;
to watch the life leave his eyes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:54.420
I think it’s Billy, because of this detail: it’s&nbsp;
still daytime when Himbry loses his life. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.180
the janitor is still here, so&nbsp;
it’s probably right after school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:00.360
too early to hang the body up at the&nbsp;
football field without getting caught.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:04.260
This means he probably had to hide the body&nbsp;
in the office somewhere and go back later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:07.800
under the fall of night, to put it on display&nbsp;
under the goalposts, which by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:11.880
is another trait similar to Michael Myers, who&nbsp;
liked to use his deceased victims as decorations.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:15.780
Stu was busy hosting his party that night,&nbsp;
and Billy doesn’t show up until later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.480
so I’m guessing that was him.&nbsp;
But before he would do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:22.860
he would have to deal with a fellow student&nbsp;
who just knew that Billy was the killer.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:26.661
(impact)

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:32.026
(rock music)

00:13:32.026 --> 00:13:35.696
Billy and Stu touch base again at Bradley&nbsp;
Video store, which is packed with people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.696 --> 00:13:39.300
due to a run in the mass murder section, and&nbsp;
the fact that people just want to stock up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:42.600
on entertainment because of the city-wide&nbsp;
curfew set to go into effect that evening.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.360
Billy can be seen flirting with&nbsp;
two girls in the horror section,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:49.320
another hint about his unfaithfulness to&nbsp;
Sidney. When he hears Randy Meeks’s yell out…

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:50.880
– EVERYBODY’S A SUSPECT!

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:53.280
– It spurs his curiosity and he goes over to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.690
confront Randy just in time to&nbsp;
hear this spot on accusation.

00:13:57.038 --> 00:13:59.402
– I’m telling you. The dad’s a red herring. It’s Billy.

00:13:59.524 --> 00:14:01.146
(stinger)

00:14:01.146 --> 00:14:03.116
– How do we know you’re not the killer?

00:14:03.116 --> 00:14:05.580
– The two real killers gang&nbsp;
up on Randy and tease him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:08.280
and although Randy really&nbsp;
suspects Billy, he can’t prove it.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:12.420
Shortly before curfew, someone in a Ghostface&nbsp;
mask stalks Sidney and Tatum in the convenience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:15.900
store. This was probably Billy, because Stu&nbsp;
would likely be setting up for the party at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:19.080
his house that evening. In any event,&nbsp;
it seems incredibly risky to just be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:22.800
wearing the Ghostface mask around in public&nbsp;
when fear of Ghostface is pretty much the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:26.100
talk of the town at this point thanks&nbsp;
to a special report by Gale Weathers.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:30.540
We actually don’t see Billy’s face again until he&nbsp;
shows up at Stu’s party, but there is the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:35.220
of who killed Tatum, who lost her life to a high&nbsp;
quality garage door opener while trying to escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.660
a Ghostface who attacked her while getting drinks&nbsp;
in the garage. Some have suggested that this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:43.680
Billy, because of how he then approaches the front&nbsp;
door from the side as if he had run out the open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:48.120
garage door and sprinted around the outside of&nbsp;
the house. It’s possible, but if that is the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:52.140
he would have had to go the long way around the&nbsp;
house, counter-clockwise. If he was coming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:55.620
the garage, it would make more sense for&nbsp;
him to go the short way around, clockwise.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:59.760
Plus, in order for him to lock Tatum into&nbsp;
the garage, then sneak in and off her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:01.740
he would have had to go through the party to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:04.680
to the door that connects the garage to&nbsp;
the house. Someone would have seen him.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:07.680
So I think it’s Stu who briefly stepped&nbsp;
out of the party and killed Tatum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.780
while Billy was taking care&nbsp;
of Himbry back at the school.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.000
Plus, Stu is the one to make this suggestion. 

00:15:12.242 --> 00:15:14.242
– Hey, Tate, grab another beer, would ya?

00:15:14.326 --> 00:15:18.060
– [Zac] Stu offers his parents bedroom for&nbsp;
Sidney and Billy to talk and… whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:20.880
and Sidney surprisingly agrees,&nbsp;
so the two head upstairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:24.420
with Billy gut punching his buddy on&nbsp;
the way for his lack of subtlety. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:28.200
perhaps it’s a celebratory smack because&nbsp;
their plan is working so flawlessly.

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.860
Upstairs, Billy apologizes for his&nbsp;
behavior lately, but to his surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.760
she’s feeling contrite as well for shutting&nbsp;
him out as she wallowed in grief over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:40.320
past year. He compares her situation to Jody&nbsp;
Foster’s character in Silence of the Lambs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:41.820
having flashbacks of her dead father.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.860
The following interaction explains&nbsp;
exactly how Billy sees their lives.

00:15:46.694 --> 00:15:50.606
– But this is life. It isn’t a movie.

00:15:50.701 --> 00:15:53.022
– Sure it is Sid. It’s all a movie.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:58.740
It’s all one great big movie.&nbsp;
Only you can’t pick your genre.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:02.460
– And with that, Sid cues him in that&nbsp;
she’s finally ready, and he plays the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:05.760
part of the supportive boyfriend, making&nbsp;
sure she’s ready before they get into it.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.640
As they get dressed, Sidney asks&nbsp;
about who he made his one phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:11.580
call to at the police station, and&nbsp;
he tells her that he called his Dad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:15.720
which puts her off slightly because she realizes&nbsp;
that Sheriff Burke was the one to call Mr. Loomis.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:19.680
– [Sidney] I was just thinking if it were you it&nbsp;
would be a very clever way to throw me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:23.820
off track. Using your one phone call to&nbsp;
call me so I wouldn’t think it was you.

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:28.200
– She seems to only be joking, but again,&nbsp;
he takes her accusation way too seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.240
making him look guiltier than he would have been.

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:34.740
– What do I have to do to prove&nbsp;
to you that I’m not a killer?

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.960
It wouldn’t take long to find out, because Stu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.560
now dressed as Ghostface comes up behind&nbsp;
Billy and butchers him with corn syrup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:43.800
packets while his back is turned away from&nbsp;
Sid, giving the appearance of real blood.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.860
From here, Billy plays dead while&nbsp;
Stu chases her though the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:51.300
and he doesn’t emerge again until he hears Sidney&nbsp;
manage to lock Stu out. Pretending to be injured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:55.740
he falls down the stairs to sell the performance.&nbsp;
Strangely, she doesn’t notice there’s only one cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.120
on his shirt where he’s supposedly been slashed,&nbsp;
yet there are two trails of corn syrup. He takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:03.840
the gun, under the ruse that he’s going to use&nbsp;
it to protect her, so when Randy butts in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:08.520
claims Stu has gone mad, Billy reveals himself as&nbsp;
the killer, by quoting Norman Bates from Psycho.

00:17:09.885 --> 00:17:13.079
– We all go a little mad sometimes!

00:17:13.278 --> 00:17:14.368
– No, Billy!

00:17:14.368 --> 00:17:18.000
– He also divulges how he used corn&nbsp;
syrup to fake his wounds, which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.940
learned from the prom scene in Carrie.&nbsp;
Like an actor giving a press interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:24.840
he’s discussing all of his influences for&nbsp;
the movie that he and Stu are creating.

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:27.540
Speaking of whom, Stu appears&nbsp;
at the doorway to the kitchen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.480
trapping Sidney in the foyer and&nbsp;
explains the nature of the game:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:35.400
she dies no matter if she wins or loses. As&nbsp;
for the rules, Sidney has broken one of them.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:42.600
– Now you're no longer a virgin.&nbsp;
 Now you gotta die; those are the rules.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:46.740
– The clock rolls past midnight and into&nbsp;
Saturday morning, the 1 year anniversary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:50.940
of Maureen’s death, so they bring out their&nbsp;
surprise -- Sidney’s Dad. They place the voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:54.480
changer and phone back in Neil’s pocket&nbsp;
to make him look like the lone killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:57.900
then they stab each other so they can&nbsp;
make themselves look like victims later.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:01.800
Billy is a little overzealous when carving&nbsp;
Stu’s wounds, going a little too deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:05.760
which starts a small argument between them. This&nbsp;
may be Billy’s psycho side taking over his body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:08.700
or it could be intentional. Now that&nbsp;
he has Sidney where he wants her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:10.620
he doesn’t really care if Stu survives or not.

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:14.520
Sidney tells them they’ve seen one too many&nbsp;
movies, which ties back to the inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:18.660
taken from the Gainesville Ripper, who many people&nbsp;
at the time believed was influenced by things he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:23.280
had seen in media. It goes back to the age old&nbsp;
question: does bad behavior in movies cause bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:28.560
behavior in real life? This was a hot topic in the&nbsp;
mid-1990s. Scream was released the same year that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:35.040
the Telecommunications Act of 1996 was signed&nbsp;
in… well… 1996. It standardized the v-chip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:38.580
a technology that allowed parents to&nbsp;
block programming based on the TV rating.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:42.060
There is always a scapegoat for the&nbsp;
Karens and Mankarens of the world to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:45.420
blame instead of just actually raising&nbsp;
your kids with proper values. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:49.740
the blame goes towards whatever is popular at&nbsp;
the time. If Scream were made in the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:52.920
it would be radio. In the 60s it would&nbsp;
have been television. In the 80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:57.780
rock and roll. In the 2000s video games and in&nbsp;
the 20s, social media. Now that I think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:01.260
there *was* a Scream movie in the 20s and&nbsp;
it’s villains *did* cite social media.

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:05.040
Is Sidney right? Has Billy Loomis&nbsp;
just seen one too many movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:08.220
causing him to snap? There have been&nbsp;
a number of studies over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:12.360
resulting in an array of conclusions,&nbsp;
which was explained by Craig Anderson, PhD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:16.980
Director of the Center for the Study of Violence&nbsp;
at Iowa State University. He said, “media violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:21.060
is only one of many risk factors for later&nbsp;
aggressive and violent behavior. Furthermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:25.620
extremely violent behavior never occurs when&nbsp;
there is only one risk factor present. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:29.700
a healthy, well-adjusted person with few risk&nbsp;
factors is not going to become a school-shooter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:33.360
just because they start playing a lot of violent&nbsp;
video games or watching a lot of violent movies.”

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:37.200
I guess Billy was kind of right, in&nbsp;
way, when he rebutted Sid’s claim.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:40.440
– No Sid! Don’t you blame the movies! Movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:44.100
don’t create psychos. Movies&nbsp;
make psychos MORE! CREATIVE!

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:48.720
The psychos would would run into an issue,&nbsp;
however, when Stu discovers that the gun is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:52.860
not where he left it, but instead in&nbsp;
the red right hand of Gale Weathers.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:55.780
(impact)

00:19:58.302 --> 00:20:01.179
(brooding music)

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:05.340
Billy Loomis now found himself to&nbsp;
be the one staring down the barrel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:09.840
which is concerning until he realizes she&nbsp;
has the safety on, giving him the confidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:13.140
to drop kick her out of the house, where&nbsp;
she’s knocked out against a support beam.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:16.860
However, before he has the chance to finish&nbsp;
her off, he’s distracted by a bigger problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:20.160
Sidney and her father have used the&nbsp;
distraction to escape from the kitchen.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:24.480
It’s strange that Billy doesn’t just pull the&nbsp;
trigger on her once the shot is lined up. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:29.160
no reason he can’t shoot her and then still go&nbsp;
look for Sidney and her father. It’s very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:33.720
something that would only happen in the movies,&nbsp;
and as we know, it’s all one great big movie.

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:37.320
However, his vision goes red when&nbsp;
he realizes he’s no longer the lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:40.980
character. The tables are about to be&nbsp;
turned on him. Sidney calls the house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.580
and claims she’s already contacted the&nbsp;
police, and Stu becomes useless when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:48.360
starts to get woozy from all of his cuts.&nbsp;
Once again, Billy has himself to blame.

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:51.600
He throws the phone at Stu and loses&nbsp;
his temper as he ransacks the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.000
cutting up couch cushions&nbsp;
to get his rage out until he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:57.180
gains enough composure to notice the&nbsp;
Halloween movie playing on the TV.

00:20:57.180 --> 00:21:01.080
As I mentioned, Halloween is the template&nbsp;
for Scream, and as the main antagonist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.380
Billy is very aware of that. He sees&nbsp;
Laurie Strode hiding in the closet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:09.120
and realizes that Sidney must be doing the same.&nbsp;
However, before he can act, she strikes first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:12.240
by stabbing him with an umbrella, causing&nbsp;
him to drop the knife and the gun.

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:15.900
He’s saved when Stu uses the last of his&nbsp;
energy to bum rush her, but she’s able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:20.040
to put him out of commission in the living room.&nbsp;
Randy comes back to, so Billy swiftly knocks him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:24.000
back out with a punch to the face and pins down&nbsp;
Sidney, telling her to say hello to her mother.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:28.260
She jabs a finger into his wound, causing him&nbsp;
to scream out and just before he can thrust his

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:32.100
knife down on her, a recently re-awakened&nbsp;
Gale Weathers shoots him in the chest.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.920
– Guess I remembered the&nbsp;
safety that time, you bastard.

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:39.360
– This might have been enough to kill a normal&nbsp;
person, but I like to believe that Billy was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:43.500
so motivated to make his life a movie, that&nbsp;
he held on just a little bit longer so that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:47.820
could have that one last scare moment that&nbsp;
he had seen in so many of his favorite films.

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:51.360
Sidney is ready for it though, and&nbsp;
delivers a headshot to put an end to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.420
the terror that plagued Woodsboro&nbsp;
over the last year. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:57.840
the threat of Billy Loomis may have&nbsp;
been gone, the horror would live on.

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:01.800
Early in this video, I described how Billy’s&nbsp;
name was inspired by a Halloween character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:06.360
whose name was inspired by a Psycho character.&nbsp;
This lineage was intentional. Billy was inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:09.840
by horror movies of the past, but now that&nbsp;
he’d modeled his life after a horror movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:12.240
he would go on to inspire&nbsp;
horror movies of the future.

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:16.080
And many people would be exposed to his story&nbsp;
when it was published in Gale Weathers’s book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:20.040
The Woodsboro Murders, later that year. The&nbsp;
book served as source material for a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:25.560
Stab, which came out the following year, in April&nbsp;
1997. Luke Wilson played the part of Billy Loomis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:28.860
and he was an even more obvious killer&nbsp;
than Skeet Ulrich was in Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:32.640
because in their world, Stab is a true crime&nbsp;
movie, so everyone already knows it’s him.

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:37.020
Around that same time, Billy’s daughter&nbsp;
Samantha Carpenter would be born. And also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:41.640
around that time, his mother would go undercover&nbsp;
as a reporter named Deborah Salt, after gaining 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:45.660
pounds and getting a makeover, in order to try&nbsp;
to get revenge on Sidney for killing her son.

00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:51.780
– You killed my son, and now I kill you,&nbsp;
I can’t think of anything more rational.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:55.560
– But the problem is, you walked out on that&nbsp;
son, so I don’t really get why you care now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:59.640
I talked about several risk factors needing to&nbsp;
be present in order to make someone go psycho,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:03.180
so I think we can safely add genetic&nbsp;
predisposition to the list for Billy.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:07.200
Mrs. Loomis isn’t successful in getting revenge&nbsp;
on Sidney either -- she seems to have that same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:10.620
cinematic reluctance to pull the trigger&nbsp;
when she probably should that her son does.

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:14.160
Mental illness continues to run down&nbsp;
the family to Samantha, who has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:17.760
take antipsychotics to calm her disturbing&nbsp;
visions of her father, which started when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:21.720
she first discovered she was related in&nbsp;
December 2010, when she was 13 years old.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:25.080
Stab became a huge franchise,&nbsp;
leading to many more little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:29.100
freaks to try to follow in the footsteps of&nbsp;
Billy Loomis. At some point in the 2010s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:34.020
Sunrise Studios released a Stab Blu-Ray box set&nbsp;
which included a special documentary called Stab:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.540
The True Story, where the real&nbsp;
Billy Loomis was featured.

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:41.760
No, you can’t actually watch that, but honestly,&nbsp;
you kind of just did. What can you can watch is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:46.080
my comprehensive analysis on the entire Stab&nbsp;
franchise. Just click that video on the left.&nbsp;&nbsp;

